1
00:00:04,450 --> 00:00:10,101
Imagine an ocean so large that you could drop every continent on Earth into it...

2
00:00:10,141 --> 00:00:12,329
and still have room to spare.

3
00:00:13,429 --> 00:00:16,319
Now imagine floating in the middle of it.

4
00:00:16,359 --> 00:00:18,678
In a rubber raft two meters wide.

5
00:00:18,718 --> 00:00:22,656
With no engine, no sail, and no way to call for help.

6
00:00:23,756 --> 00:00:29,424
Around you, in every direction, the horizon is perfectly flat. Perfectly empty.

7
00:00:29,464 --> 00:00:33,732
And the nearest land could be a thousand kilometers away.

8
00:00:34,832 --> 00:00:36,681
This is the story of Noah.

9
00:00:36,721 --> 00:00:40,505
And of the thirty-eight days he spent lost at sea.

10
00:00:50,105 --> 00:00:55,555
After the Amazon, Noah swore he would never again say yes to an adventure without

11
00:00:55,595 --> 00:00:57,372
thinking about it first.

12
00:00:58,472 --> 00:01:00,430
He lasted four months.

13
00:01:01,530 --> 00:01:06,742
The offer came from a sixty-three-year-old Frenchman named Leon Marchand,

14
00:01:06,782 --> 00:01:10,842
a retired engineer who had sailed around the world twice,

15
00:01:10,882 --> 00:01:16,669
and needed one extra crew member to help him deliver his yacht across the Pacific

16
00:01:16,709 --> 00:01:17,291
Ocean.

17
00:01:18,391 --> 00:01:19,809
Six thousand kilometers.

18
00:01:19,849 --> 00:01:21,753
Panama to the Marquesas Islands.

19
00:01:21,793 --> 00:01:25,832
Four weeks of ocean, stars, and terrible coffee. You'll love it.

20
00:01:26,932 --> 00:01:28,728
The yacht was called Wren.

21
00:01:28,768 --> 00:01:31,764
Twelve meters long, white hull, blue sails,

22
00:01:31,804 --> 00:01:35,555
and a small cabin that smelled of varnish and salt.

23
00:01:36,655 --> 00:01:42,428
For the first two weeks, it was exactly what Leon had promised. Endless blue water.

24
00:01:42,468 --> 00:01:44,109
Dolphins racing the bow.

25
00:01:44,149 --> 00:01:48,502
Nights so clear that the Milky Way cast shadows on the deck.

26
00:01:49,602 --> 00:01:52,608
The ocean doesn't care about you, Noah. Not in a cruel way.

27
00:01:52,648 --> 00:01:54,105
It simply doesn't notice you.

28
00:01:54,145 --> 00:01:55,706
That's what makes it beautiful.

29
00:01:55,746 --> 00:01:57,703
And that's what makes it dangerous.

30
00:01:58,803 --> 00:02:03,182
Leon taught him how to steer by the stars. How to read the swell.

31
00:02:03,222 --> 00:02:08,620
And where everything was kept, in case of an emergency. Especially the grab bag.

32
00:02:08,660 --> 00:02:12,019
The orange waterproof bag beside the companionway,

33
00:02:12,059 --> 00:02:16,492
with everything you would need if you ever had to abandon ship.

34
00:02:17,592 --> 00:02:20,396
Has it ever happened to you? Abandoning ship?

35
00:02:21,496 --> 00:02:22,797
Never. In forty years.

36
00:02:22,837 --> 00:02:25,241
And I would like to keep it that way.

37
00:02:30,341 --> 00:02:35,713
On the sixteenth night, more than two thousand kilometers from the nearest land,

38
00:02:35,753 --> 00:02:38,676
Noah was on watch while Leon slept below.

39
00:02:39,776 --> 00:02:42,184
The sea was calm. The wind was steady.

40
00:02:42,224 --> 00:02:45,209
There was nothing out there. Nothing at all.

41
00:02:46,309 --> 00:02:52,005
Every year, cargo ships lose thousands of steel shipping containers overboard in

42
00:02:52,045 --> 00:02:53,295
storms. Some sink.

43
00:02:53,335 --> 00:02:58,959
But some float for weeks or months, just below the surface. Invisible at night.

44
00:02:58,999 --> 00:03:00,583
As heavy as a truck.

45
00:03:03,683 --> 00:03:08,532
The impact threw Noah across the cockpit. Below, he heard Leon shout.

46
00:03:08,572 --> 00:03:13,776
And then he heard the sound every sailor fears more than any storm. Water.

47
00:03:13,816 --> 00:03:14,745
Rushing in.

48
00:03:15,845 --> 00:03:18,574
The grab bag! Get the raft! Go, go, go!

49
00:03:19,674 --> 00:03:24,533
Leon threw the life raft canister over the side and pulled the cord.

50
00:03:24,573 --> 00:03:30,126
With a roar of compressed air, an orange shape unfolded on the black water.

51
00:03:31,226 --> 00:03:33,517
Wren was sinking by the bow. Fast.

52
00:03:33,557 --> 00:03:37,358
Noah jumped into the raft with the grab bag in his arms.

53
00:03:37,398 --> 00:03:40,702
Leon went back below for the satellite beacon.

54
00:03:41,802 --> 00:03:44,486
And then the mast came down between them,

55
00:03:44,526 --> 00:03:47,542
and a wave pushed the raft away from the hull.

56
00:03:47,582 --> 00:03:49,800
Noah heard Leon shouting his name.

57
00:03:49,840 --> 00:03:55,504
He saw a flashlight in the water, then a second, smaller shape. The yacht's dinghy.

58
00:03:56,604 --> 00:03:58,622
Leon! Leon! Over here! Leon!

59
00:03:59,722 --> 00:04:02,415
In four minutes, the yacht was gone.

60
00:04:02,455 --> 00:04:06,743
In ten, Leon's flashlight was a tiny star on the horizon.

61
00:04:06,783 --> 00:04:08,983
In twenty, it was gone too.

62
00:04:10,083 --> 00:04:11,159
Noah was alone.

63
00:04:11,199 --> 00:04:14,622
In the middle of the largest ocean on Earth.

64
00:04:19,722 --> 00:04:25,650
At dawn, he opened the grab bag and laid everything out on the floor of the raft.

65
00:04:25,690 --> 00:04:27,535
Every item. One by one.

66
00:04:28,635 --> 00:04:32,067
Six liters of fresh water, in sealed pouches.

67
00:04:32,107 --> 00:04:37,929
Twelve packets of emergency ration biscuits. A fishing kit. A signal mirror.

68
00:04:37,969 --> 00:04:43,561
Three red parachute flares. A knife. A small hand pump. A plastic bailer.

69
00:04:43,601 --> 00:04:47,994
And a folded device he didn't recognize. A solar still.

70
00:04:49,094 --> 00:04:53,676
What wasn't there was the satellite beacon. Leon had gone back for it.

71
00:04:53,716 --> 00:04:56,970
Wherever Leon was now, the beacon was with him.

72
00:04:58,070 --> 00:04:59,662
Day one. I'm in the raft.

73
00:04:59,702 --> 00:05:03,513
Leon's somewhere out there in the dinghy. I hope he's okay.

74
00:05:03,553 --> 00:05:05,340
I hope he has the beacon on.

75
00:05:05,380 --> 00:05:08,076
I hope someone's coming for both of us.

76
00:05:09,176 --> 00:05:13,417
The raft was a circle of orange rubber with an arched canopy,

77
00:05:13,457 --> 00:05:17,488
just big enough for him to lie down if he curled his legs.

78
00:05:17,528 --> 00:05:23,504
It rose and fell with every swell, endlessly, like the breathing of a giant animal.

79
00:05:24,604 --> 00:05:26,862
On the first day, he was seasick.

80
00:05:26,902 --> 00:05:29,090
On the second, he was sunburned.

81
00:05:29,130 --> 00:05:34,223
On the third, the inside of the raft smelled of rubber, sweat and fear.

82
00:05:35,323 --> 00:05:37,030
The nights were the worst.

83
00:05:37,070 --> 00:05:39,180
In the dark, the sea was louder.

84
00:05:39,220 --> 00:05:44,554
Every slap of a wave against the rubber sounded like something trying to get in.

85
00:05:44,594 --> 00:05:49,850
And sometimes, in the wind, he was sure he could hear Leon calling his name.

86
00:05:50,950 --> 00:05:53,538
He knew one thing from Leon's lessons.

87
00:05:53,578 --> 00:05:59,400
People lost at sea are not usually killed by the ocean. They are killed by thirst.

88
00:06:04,500 --> 00:06:07,903
A human being can survive for weeks without food.

89
00:06:07,943 --> 00:06:13,083
But without water, in the heat of the tropics, only three or four days.

90
00:06:14,183 --> 00:06:19,214
And the cruelest thing about the ocean is that you are surrounded by water you

91
00:06:19,254 --> 00:06:20,059
cannot drink.

92
00:06:20,099 --> 00:06:23,830
Seawater is more than three times saltier than your blood.

93
00:06:23,870 --> 00:06:28,311
Drinking it makes you thirstier, then confused, then it kills you.

94
00:06:29,411 --> 00:06:34,098
Noah rationed his six liters to half a liter a day. It was not enough.

95
00:06:34,138 --> 00:06:37,070
His lips cracked. His head ached constantly.

96
00:06:37,110 --> 00:06:40,366
He tried not to move during the hottest hours.

97
00:06:41,466 --> 00:06:47,118
He did the math every morning, the way he used to do it in the bunker. Six liters.

98
00:06:47,158 --> 00:06:51,265
Half a liter a day. Twelve days. And after that, nothing.

99
00:06:52,365 --> 00:06:54,855
Then he figured out the solar still.

100
00:06:54,895 --> 00:06:58,580
A small inflatable cone that floated beside the raft.

101
00:06:58,620 --> 00:07:01,250
Seawater went into a black pad inside.

102
00:07:01,290 --> 00:07:06,802
The sun heated it, fresh water evaporated, condensed on the clear plastic dome,

103
00:07:06,842 --> 00:07:09,733
and dripped into a pouch at the bottom.

104
00:07:10,833 --> 00:07:13,554
On a good day, it made almost a liter.

105
00:07:13,594 --> 00:07:16,069
On a cloudy day, almost nothing.

106
00:07:17,169 --> 00:07:20,134
And then, on the ninth day, it rained.

107
00:07:21,234 --> 00:07:25,940
Noah stripped off his shirt and stood in the downpour with his mouth open,

108
00:07:25,980 --> 00:07:27,415
laughing like a madman.

109
00:07:27,455 --> 00:07:32,674
He caught the rain in the raft's canopy, channeled it into every container he had,

110
00:07:32,714 --> 00:07:34,980
and drank until his stomach hurt.

111
00:07:36,080 --> 00:07:39,951
Day nine. Rain. Real rain. I filled everything. I'm rich.

112
00:07:39,991 --> 00:07:42,955
I'm the richest man in the Pacific Ocean.

113
00:07:48,055 --> 00:07:52,912
On the twelfth morning, Noah woke up to a sound he had almost forgotten.

114
00:07:52,952 --> 00:07:55,415
The deep, steady hum of an engine.

115
00:07:56,515 --> 00:08:00,401
On the horizon, a container ship. Enormous. Dark red.

116
00:08:00,441 --> 00:08:05,036
Moving slowly from left to right, maybe two kilometers away.

117
00:08:06,136 --> 00:08:07,981
Here! Here! Over here! Please!

118
00:08:09,081 --> 00:08:10,790
He fired the first flare.

119
00:08:10,830 --> 00:08:15,616
A ball of red fire arced into the sky, so bright he had to look away.

120
00:08:15,656 --> 00:08:19,304
In the morning sunlight, it looked small and pale.

121
00:08:20,404 --> 00:08:24,931
He grabbed the signal mirror and flashed it toward the ship's bridge,

122
00:08:24,971 --> 00:08:28,034
again and again, the way Leon had shown him.

123
00:08:29,134 --> 00:08:34,019
The ship kept moving. It didn't slow down. It didn't change course.

124
00:08:35,119 --> 00:08:39,081
Modern cargo ships are run by crews of twenty people,

125
00:08:39,121 --> 00:08:43,083
working on a vessel the size of four football fields.

126
00:08:43,123 --> 00:08:47,161
At sea, often only one person is watching the horizon.

127
00:08:47,201 --> 00:08:52,184
And a small orange raft, low in the waves, is very easy to miss.

128
00:08:53,284 --> 00:08:55,322
In an hour, the ship was gone.

129
00:08:55,362 --> 00:09:00,655
Noah sat in the raft and stared at the empty horizon, and for the first time,

130
00:09:00,695 --> 00:09:04,031
he let himself believe that no one was coming.

131
00:09:05,131 --> 00:09:08,024
Day twelve. A ship passed. It didn't see me.

132
00:09:08,064 --> 00:09:12,091
I screamed at it for an hour after it was gone. Then I cried.

133
00:09:12,131 --> 00:09:14,024
And then I decided something.

134
00:09:14,064 --> 00:09:17,681
If nobody is coming, then I have to get myself home.

135
00:09:22,781 --> 00:09:27,242
But the ocean, he slowly discovered, was not empty at all.

136
00:09:28,342 --> 00:09:32,521
By the second week, a small world had formed beneath the raft.

137
00:09:32,561 --> 00:09:35,107
Little striped fish hid in its shadow.

138
00:09:35,147 --> 00:09:37,625
Bigger fish followed the little ones.

139
00:09:37,665 --> 00:09:39,598
And then the dorados arrived.

140
00:09:39,638 --> 00:09:45,232
Golden-green, fast, and curious, bumping against the floor of the raft at night.

141
00:09:46,332 --> 00:09:50,309
With the fishing kit, Noah caught one on the fifteenth day.

142
00:09:50,349 --> 00:09:53,918
It was the first real meal he had eaten in two weeks.

143
00:09:53,958 --> 00:09:59,351
He ate it raw, in thin strips, the way Leon had told him the old sailors did.

144
00:10:00,451 --> 00:10:05,398
Flying fish crashed into the canopy at night, attracted by nothing at all.

145
00:10:05,438 --> 00:10:09,902
In the morning, he found them on the raft. Breakfast, delivered.

146
00:10:11,002 --> 00:10:16,200
One morning, a sea turtle surfaced beside the raft, as big as a coffee table,

147
00:10:16,240 --> 00:10:19,397
and looked at him with ancient, unhurried eyes.

148
00:10:19,437 --> 00:10:22,186
It stayed for a minute, breathing slowly.

149
00:10:22,226 --> 00:10:27,410
Then it sank back into the blue, as if it had simply come to check on him.

150
00:10:28,510 --> 00:10:30,578
And then came the sharks.

151
00:10:31,678 --> 00:10:32,855
Oceanic whitetips.

152
00:10:32,895 --> 00:10:37,383
Two of them, three meters long, circling slowly, patient as clocks.

153
00:10:37,423 --> 00:10:41,573
Sometimes one would rub its back along the bottom of the raft,

154
00:10:41,613 --> 00:10:45,278
and the whole floor would ripple under Noah's knees.

155
00:10:46,378 --> 00:10:47,647
They never attacked.

156
00:10:47,687 --> 00:10:50,329
A raft doesn't look like food to a shark.

157
00:10:50,369 --> 00:10:53,594
But Noah never put his hand in the water again.

158
00:10:54,694 --> 00:10:56,494
At night, the ocean glowed.

159
00:10:56,534 --> 00:11:01,604
Every wave that broke against the raft lit up in sparks of blue-green fire.

160
00:11:01,644 --> 00:11:05,488
Tiny organisms called plankton, flashing their own light.

161
00:11:05,528 --> 00:11:10,993
When Noah dragged his fingers along the surface, they left trails like comets.

162
00:11:12,093 --> 00:11:16,068
Day seventeen. The ocean is glowing. I'm watching it right now.

163
00:11:16,108 --> 00:11:20,272
I wish someone else could see this. I wish Leon could see this.

164
00:11:25,372 --> 00:11:29,609
On the twenty-second day, the sky turned the color of a bruise.

165
00:11:30,709 --> 00:11:32,985
The swells grew taller than houses.

166
00:11:33,025 --> 00:11:37,750
The raft climbed each one slowly, hung at the top for a terrible second,

167
00:11:37,790 --> 00:11:41,447
and then slid down into the valley on the other side.

168
00:11:42,547 --> 00:11:44,438
Noah zipped the canopy shut,

169
00:11:44,478 --> 00:11:49,661
tied himself to the inside of the raft with the safety line, and held on.

170
00:11:50,761 --> 00:11:55,187
In the middle of the night, a breaking wave caught the raft sideways...

171
00:11:55,227 --> 00:11:57,075
and flipped it upside down.

172
00:12:00,175 --> 00:12:04,342
He was underwater. Inside the raft. In complete darkness.

173
00:12:04,382 --> 00:12:08,813
Tangled in the safety line, with no idea which way was up.

174
00:12:09,913 --> 00:12:13,080
He forced himself to stop. To feel for the line.

175
00:12:13,120 --> 00:12:17,423
To follow it, hand over hand, to the edge of the raft... and out.

176
00:12:17,463 --> 00:12:23,092
He surfaced into the screaming wind, gasping, with the overturned raft beside him.

177
00:12:24,192 --> 00:12:27,268
There was a strap on the bottom, for exactly this.

178
00:12:27,308 --> 00:12:31,755
He climbed onto the raft, grabbed the strap, threw his weight backwards,

179
00:12:31,795 --> 00:12:32,939
and pulled it over.

180
00:12:32,979 --> 00:12:34,936
On the third try, it flipped.

181
00:12:36,036 --> 00:12:40,569
When the storm passed the next afternoon, he counted what was left.

182
00:12:40,609 --> 00:12:42,275
The solar still was gone.

183
00:12:42,315 --> 00:12:47,788
Half of the rain water was gone. Two packets of biscuits. And the fishing kit.

184
00:12:48,888 --> 00:12:53,620
Day twenty-three. I lost almost everything. But I'm still here. I'm still here.

185
00:12:53,660 --> 00:12:56,105
Do you hear me, ocean? I'm still here.

186
00:13:01,205 --> 00:13:03,798
Two days later, he had a visitor.

187
00:13:04,898 --> 00:13:10,202
It came out of the sky like a glider, without a single wingbeat. White body.

188
00:13:10,242 --> 00:13:14,681
Dark wings, each one longer than Noah was tall. An albatross.

189
00:13:15,781 --> 00:13:20,474
It circled the raft three times, then landed on the water beside it,

190
00:13:20,514 --> 00:13:23,607
folded its enormous wings, and looked at him.

191
00:13:23,647 --> 00:13:26,024
As if it had been expecting him.

192
00:13:27,124 --> 00:13:29,298
Hello. Hello there. Are you lost too?

193
00:13:30,398 --> 00:13:34,908
The bird stayed for an hour. The next day, it came back. And the next.

194
00:13:34,948 --> 00:13:39,784
It would sit on the edge of the raft in the morning, preening its feathers,

195
00:13:39,824 --> 00:13:41,274
completely unafraid.

196
00:13:42,374 --> 00:13:43,882
I'm going to call you Leon.

197
00:13:43,922 --> 00:13:45,487
You're old, you're stubborn,

198
00:13:45,527 --> 00:13:49,920
and you showed up out of nowhere in the middle of the ocean. It suits you.

199
00:13:51,020 --> 00:13:56,801
On the third visit, Noah noticed something on its leg. A small metal band. A ring.

200
00:13:56,841 --> 00:13:59,050
With letters stamped into it.

201
00:14:00,150 --> 00:14:03,696
He leaned closer, very slowly. The bird let him.

202
00:14:03,736 --> 00:14:09,339
The letters read: Tamaru Atoll Research. Return to station. And a number.

203
00:14:10,439 --> 00:14:12,566
A research station. On an atoll.

204
00:14:12,606 --> 00:14:17,022
Somewhere close enough for an albatross to visit him every day.

205
00:14:18,122 --> 00:14:21,107
Albatrosses can fly thousands of kilometers.

206
00:14:21,147 --> 00:14:26,607
But during the nesting season, they return to the same island, to the same mate,

207
00:14:26,647 --> 00:14:27,707
again and again.

208
00:14:27,747 --> 00:14:31,350
And Noah remembered something else Leon had told him,

209
00:14:31,390 --> 00:14:33,757
on one of those long night watches.

210
00:14:33,797 --> 00:14:36,284
In the evening, seabirds fly home.

211
00:14:41,384 --> 00:14:45,642
That evening, Noah watched the albatross take off from the raft.

212
00:14:45,682 --> 00:14:48,395
It climbed into the wind, circled once...

213
00:14:48,435 --> 00:14:53,621
and then flew in a straight line, low over the water, toward the southwest.

214
00:14:54,721 --> 00:14:59,156
The next evening, it did the same. Southwest. And the next.

215
00:15:00,256 --> 00:15:01,959
A life raft has no sail.

216
00:15:01,999 --> 00:15:05,589
It goes wherever the wind and the current take it.

217
00:15:05,629 --> 00:15:10,209
But Noah had a knife, a paddle, and the raft's orange canopy.

218
00:15:11,309 --> 00:15:16,279
He cut the canopy free on one side, lashed it between the two paddles,

219
00:15:16,319 --> 00:15:18,282
and raised it into the wind.

220
00:15:18,322 --> 00:15:24,151
It was the ugliest sail in the history of the Pacific. But the raft began to move.

221
00:15:24,191 --> 00:15:25,629
Slowly. Southwest.

222
00:15:26,729 --> 00:15:29,904
Every evening, the albatross showed him the way.

223
00:15:29,944 --> 00:15:33,722
Every night, he steered by the stars Leon had taught him.

224
00:15:33,762 --> 00:15:37,261
Every morning, he corrected his course by the sun.

225
00:15:38,361 --> 00:15:42,147
At night, he kept the Southern Cross on his left shoulder,

226
00:15:42,187 --> 00:15:46,104
exactly the way Leon had shown him from the cockpit of Wren.

227
00:15:46,144 --> 00:15:49,600
In the dark, alone, with the stars wheeling overhead,

228
00:15:49,640 --> 00:15:51,843
he could almost hear Leon's voice.

229
00:15:51,883 --> 00:15:55,133
A little more to the west, Noah. A little more.

230
00:15:56,233 --> 00:15:58,406
Day thirty-one. Following a bird.

231
00:15:58,446 --> 00:16:03,571
If anyone ever finds this recording, I want you to know I am completely sane.

232
00:16:03,611 --> 00:16:05,505
I'm just following a bird.

233
00:16:06,605 --> 00:16:08,034
His body was failing.

234
00:16:08,074 --> 00:16:11,393
He had lost so much weight that his ribs showed.

235
00:16:11,433 --> 00:16:13,352
Salt sores covered his skin.

236
00:16:13,392 --> 00:16:15,452
He slept fourteen hours a day.

237
00:16:15,492 --> 00:16:19,720
But the water lasted, just barely, thanks to two more storms.

238
00:16:19,760 --> 00:16:22,569
And every evening, the bird came back.

239
00:16:27,669 --> 00:16:32,868
On the thirty-sixth day, Noah saw something strange on the horizon. A cloud.

240
00:16:32,908 --> 00:16:34,247
Not like the others.

241
00:16:34,287 --> 00:16:39,263
It sat perfectly still, while every other cloud in the sky was moving.

242
00:16:40,363 --> 00:16:43,876
Sailors have known about this for thousands of years.

243
00:16:43,916 --> 00:16:46,758
Clouds form above islands, even small ones,

244
00:16:46,798 --> 00:16:50,512
because the land heats the air differently than the sea.

245
00:16:50,552 --> 00:16:54,456
Long before you can see the land, you can see its cloud.

246
00:16:55,556 --> 00:16:58,435
The next morning, the water changed color.

247
00:16:58,475 --> 00:17:02,258
From deep, endless blue to a bright, glowing turquoise.

248
00:17:02,298 --> 00:17:08,008
And Noah smelled something he hadn't smelled in five weeks. Plants. Earth. Land.

249
00:17:09,108 --> 00:17:12,319
And then he saw it. A thin line of white sand.

250
00:17:12,359 --> 00:17:15,004
A few palm trees, leaning in the wind.

251
00:17:15,044 --> 00:17:19,999
And beyond them, a small white building with an antenna on its roof.

252
00:17:21,099 --> 00:17:23,210
Land. Land. Oh my God. Land!

253
00:17:24,310 --> 00:17:27,547
But between the raft and the beach was a reef.

254
00:17:27,587 --> 00:17:30,326
A wall of coral just below the surface,

255
00:17:30,366 --> 00:17:34,149
with waves exploding on it in white sheets of foam.

256
00:17:35,249 --> 00:17:36,893
There was a gap. Just one.

257
00:17:36,933 --> 00:17:40,975
Where the water ran smooth and dark between the breaking waves.

258
00:17:41,015 --> 00:17:45,510
Noah paddled with everything he had left, aimed the raft at the gap...

259
00:17:45,550 --> 00:17:48,291
and let the next wave carry him through.

260
00:17:49,391 --> 00:17:54,241
The raft scraped over the coral, spun once, and slid into the calm,

261
00:17:54,281 --> 00:17:56,402
glassy water of the lagoon.

262
00:18:01,502 --> 00:18:04,595
The researcher who found him was named Hana.

263
00:18:04,635 --> 00:18:07,302
She was twenty-nine, from New Zealand,

264
00:18:07,342 --> 00:18:12,572
and she had spent six months on Tamaru Atoll studying albatrosses with two

265
00:18:12,612 --> 00:18:13,545
colleagues.

266
00:18:14,645 --> 00:18:18,030
Oh my God. Are you okay? Can you hear me? Where did you come from?

267
00:18:19,130 --> 00:18:21,195
Your bird. I followed your bird.

268
00:18:22,295 --> 00:18:24,940
At first, Hana thought he was delirious.

269
00:18:24,980 --> 00:18:29,170
Then he described the band on its leg. She checked her records.

270
00:18:29,210 --> 00:18:31,856
Albatross number four-seven-one. A male.

271
00:18:31,896 --> 00:18:37,618
Who had been coming back to his nest every evening, late, for the past three weeks.

272
00:18:38,718 --> 00:18:40,722
He was visiting you. Every single day.

273
00:18:40,762 --> 00:18:42,981
Then flying three hundred kilometers home.

274
00:18:43,021 --> 00:18:45,322
I thought he was just going far to fish.

275
00:18:46,422 --> 00:18:51,760
Hana and her two colleagues carried him to the station, wrapped him in a sheet,

276
00:18:51,800 --> 00:18:56,458
and gave him a glass of cold fresh water. He held it with both hands.

277
00:18:56,498 --> 00:19:01,345
It was, he said later, the best thing he had ever tasted in his life.

278
00:19:02,445 --> 00:19:04,890
The station had a satellite phone.

279
00:19:04,930 --> 00:19:09,933
The first call Noah made was to the maritime rescue center in Tahiti.

280
00:19:09,973 --> 00:19:14,216
The second was to his sister. The third was the hardest.

281
00:19:15,316 --> 00:19:16,325
Leon was alive.

282
00:19:16,365 --> 00:19:20,101
A fishing boat had found his dinghy on the fourth day,

283
00:19:20,141 --> 00:19:24,437
three hundred kilometers away, with the beacon still flashing.

284
00:19:24,477 --> 00:19:29,681
He had spent the next five weeks in a hotel in Tahiti, refusing to go home,

285
00:19:29,721 --> 00:19:33,158
calling the rescue center every single morning.

286
00:19:34,258 --> 00:19:35,808
Noah? Noah. Thirty-eight days.

287
00:19:35,848 --> 00:19:37,293
They told me to stop hoping.

288
00:19:37,333 --> 00:19:39,232
I told them they didn't know you.

289
00:19:40,332 --> 00:19:43,679
A week later, a supply ship came to Tamaru Atoll.

290
00:19:43,719 --> 00:19:45,753
Leon was standing on its deck.

291
00:19:45,793 --> 00:19:50,177
When it reached the lagoon, he didn't wait for the boat to dock.

292
00:19:50,217 --> 00:19:54,376
He jumped into the water, sixty-three years old, and swam.

293
00:19:55,476 --> 00:19:59,445
Noah lost fifteen kilos during his thirty-eight days at sea.

294
00:19:59,485 --> 00:20:01,450
It took him months to recover.

295
00:20:01,490 --> 00:20:03,789
He still can't eat ration biscuits.

296
00:20:03,829 --> 00:20:06,785
And he has never been on a sailboat again.

297
00:20:07,885 --> 00:20:13,835
But every year, Hana sends him an email. Just one line. Four-seven-one is back.

298
00:20:14,935 --> 00:20:19,902
Because sometimes, when you are lost in the largest, emptiest place on Earth...

299
00:20:19,942 --> 00:20:23,895
help comes from the last place you would ever think to look.

300
00:20:29,995 --> 00:20:33,568
If you were lost at sea, and a bird showed up every evening,

301
00:20:33,608 --> 00:20:36,700
flying in the same direction... would you follow it?

302
00:20:36,740 --> 00:20:41,647
Or would you stay where the rescuers might be looking? Tell us in the comments.

303
00:20:42,747 --> 00:20:43,791
And remember...

304
00:20:43,831 --> 00:20:46,798
when everything ends, the story begins.
